// @trap
// A COMPARATOR THAT SHRINKS THE RECEIVER MAKES `sort` TRAP. This is the sharpest
// difference between `std:array`'s two ordering exports, and it is pinned rather than
// merely described because an earlier draft of the module header asserted the opposite —
// that `sort` "cannot trap" because it captures `n` once. Capturing `n` is exactly what
// makes it trap: `pop()` is expressible on a VL list, so after the comparator pops, an
// index below the captured `n` is out of bounds.
//
// The mechanism, in order:
//   1. `sort` reads `const n = self.length` — 40 — and never re-reads it.
//   2. The base case and the merge index `self[...]` freely below `n`.
//   3. The comparator pops on its 5th call, so the backing store is now 39 long.
//   4. The next read at index 39 is out of range and the engine traps.
//
// `sorted` is IMMUNE to the same comparator, and that is not luck — it fills its working
// buffer and reads `n` from the copy before `less` is ever called, so the receiver is no
// longer load-bearing. `array-sort-inplace.vl` is the inverted control that shows it
// returning a correct sort of all 40 original elements while the receiver ends at 39;
// this file cannot show it, because the harness ignores `@log` on a `@trap` case.
//
// NEITHER EXPORT DEFENDS AGAINST THIS, and neither should — a per-access length re-read
// would cost the hot loop for a caller error. What the API owes is a stated consequence,
// which the module header now carries.
//
// NOTE the length: 40 crosses SORT_RUN = 16, so the pop lands during the base case and
// the trap is reached without the merge ever running. A shorter list would trap too; this
// one keeps the mechanism above the simplest.
import { sort } from "std:array"

const xs: i32[] = []
let i = 0
while i < 40 {
  xs.push((i * 7) % 40)
  i = i + 1
}

let calls = 0
xs.sort((a: i32, b: i32) => {
  calls = calls + 1
  if calls == 5 { xs.pop() }
  a < b
})
print(xs.length)
